gregs@eskimo.com (Greg Sumner) writes:

>In article <2mvi7m$h15@news.iastate.edu>,
>James R. Goodfriend <phaedrus@iastate.edu> wrote:
>>	I use an easier way to kill the altar priest every time.

>It seems to me that this should be changed in future versions.  Why
>would a Priest stay behind to guard an altar that is no longer his?
>Once you've desecrated the place, he should chase you down for
>revenge.  By making him stay in the room, he's too easy to kill.

Perhaps he should kill the nearest monster (which *may* be the player,
but may not) and use its corpse to reconvert the altar.  This would
force the player to kill him, rather than run away, or risk losing the
freshly-converted altar.  If the priest picks a wimpy monster, *he*
may be converted to your alignment!

I can just picture the player converting an altar over and over,
and running away each time, until the priest gets converted!  Is
there any real advantage to a like-aligned priest?  Of course,
a living, friendly priest is always better than an angry one, but
is a living, co-aligned priest (source of AC, etc.) better than a
dead non-aligned priest (source of armor, books, etc.)?
